      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;A/&beta;S females may be bred to h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;A/&beta;S males, h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;A/&beta;A males, or with h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;S/&beta;S males. These mice should never be bred with wildtype mice or the humanized genes may be lost. Also, h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;S/&beta;S females are poor mothers and should not be used for sustaining a live colony. Mating scheme: Homozygous for Hbatm1(HBA)Tow, Compound Heterozygous for Hbbtm2(HBG1,HBB*)Tow/Hbbtm3(HBG1,HBB)Tow x Homozygous for Hbatm1(HBA)Tow, Homozygous for Hbbtm3(HBG1,HBB)Tow or Homozygous for Hbatm1(HBA)Tow, Compound Heterozygous for Hbbtm2(HBG1,HBB*)Tow/Hbbtm3(HBG1,HBB)Tow x Homozygous for Hbatm1(HBA)Tow, Compound Heterozygous for Hbbtm2(HBG1,HBB*)Tow/Hbbtm3(HBG1,HBB)Tow Coat color: Various Generation: Contact Technical Support",
      "detailed_desc": "These mice harbor a combination of the following knock-in mutations: 1) the Hbatm1(HBA)Tow mutation (also called h&alpha; ; replaces the endogenous mouse &alpha;-globin with the human &alpha;-globin gene), which is necessary to form the hemoglobin heterotetramer in conjunction with: 2) the Hbbtm2(HBG1,HBB*)Tow mutation (also called -1400 &gamma;-&beta;S ; replaces the endogenous mouse major and minor &beta;-globin with the human hemoglobin gamma (A&gamma;) gene and the human sickle cell hemoglobin beta (&beta;S) gene), 3) the Hbbtm3(HBG1,HBB)Tow mutation (also called -383 &gamma;-&beta;A ; replacing the endogenous mouse major and minor &beta;-globin with the human hemoglobin gamma (A&gamma;) gene and the human wildtype hemoglobin beta (&beta;A) gene). Mice homozygous for Hbatm1(HBA)Tow and homozygous for Hbbtm2(HBG1,HBB*)Tow express the sickle cell disease phenotype and are referred to as h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;S/&beta;S. Mice homozygous for Hbatm1(HBA)Tow and homozygous for Hbbtm3(HBG1,HBB)Tow are phenotypically normal. These mice are referred to as h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;A/&beta;A and may be used as controls. The presence of one copy of the Hbbtm3(HBG1,HBB)Tow allele is sufficient to rescue the sickle cell phenotype. Therefore, mice homozygous for Hbatm1(HBA)Tow and heterozygous for Hbbtm2(HBG1,HBB*)Tow and Hbbtm3(HBG1,HBB)Tow (compound heterozygous) are also phenotypically normal and may be used for breeding, or as controls. These mice are referred to as h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;A/&beta;S. *Of note, none of the animals offered from this strain carry the mouse wildtype allele at either the Hba or Hbb loci* The h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;S/&beta;S, the h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;A/&beta;A, and the h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;A/&beta;S mice are all viable and fertile, though h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;S/&beta;S females are very poor mothers. When carrying two copies of the &beta;S allele (i.e., h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;S/&beta;S), mice develop a human sickle cell disease phenotype. Many rigid, elongated, sickle-shaped red blood cells (RBCs) are seen in blood smears from h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;S/&beta;S mice. The spleens of h&alpha;/h&alpha;::&beta;S/&beta;S mice exhibit expansion of red pulp, pooling of sinusoidal erythrocytes, vasoocclusion, and a complete loss of lymphoid follicular structure. Focal areas of necrosis, hemosiderin deposition, and congestion of the vasculature with aggregates of sickled RBCs is evident in the liver. Erythroid progenitors are "
