      "breeding_summary": "Animals homozygous for all seven of the mutations (Il2rg is X-linked) are viable and fertile, but severely immunocompromised. Maintenance under specific pathogen-free conditions promotes overall colony health. If maintained in low health barrier rooms, the use of medicated water (e.g., sulfatrim/trimethoprim-sulfa or enrofloxacin/Baytril) is recommended. Also see additional NSG housing information. Enrichment items (e.g., Shepherd Shacks, nesting materials) and reduced housing density may reduce any aggressive behaviors. Generation: F8",
      "detailed_desc": "S15-DKO (NSG&reg;-SGM3-IL15-MHC I/II DKO) mice combine the features of the highly immunodeficient NSG mouse (deficient in B, T, and NK cells as well as C5 complement) with an MHC class I/II double knock-out and the expression of human cytokines IL3 (interleukin 3), CSF2 (colony stimulating factor 2; GM-CSF), KITLG (KIT ligand; SCF), and IL15 (interleukin 15) that support the stable engraftment of myeloid lineages, regulatory T cell populations and NK cells. S15-DKO is the next iteration of the NSG-MHC I/II DKO mouse (Stock No. 025216) with genetic modifications that promote a more comprehensive immune cell repertoire following peripheral blood mononuclear cell (PBMC) engraftment, including myeloid, natural killer, gamma delta T, and mature B cells. This model enables researchers to evaluate therapies such as T cell engagers or CAR T in a more complex human immune model and allows for cell engagement of immune cell subtypes such as myeloid or memory B cells that were not possible in previous cell humanized mouse models. This addresses a major unmet need within the immuno-oncology space. Animals homozygous for all seven of the mutations (Il2rg is X-linked) are viable and fertile, but severely immunocompromised. Maintenance under specific pathogen-free conditions promotes overall colony health. If maintained in low health barrier rooms, the use of medicated water (e.g., sulfatrim/trimethoprim-sulfa or enrofloxacin/Baytril) is recommended. Also see additional NSG housing information. Enrichment items (e.g., Shepherd Shacks, nesting materials) and reduced housing density may reduce any aggressive behaviors."
