      "breeding_summary": "Mice homozygous for the mutation are viable and fertile and can be used for breeding. Mating scheme: Homozygote x Homozygote Generation: N5+pN1F3",
      "detailed_desc": "&alpha;-Synuclein is a neuronal protein (coded by Snca) which plays a role in synaptic plasticity and neurotransmitter release. Mutations of Snca have been implicated in a group of neurodegenerative diseases that result from an accumulation of &alpha;-synuclein in neurons or, in some cases, glial cells. These disorders include Parkinson's disease (PDD), dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB), the Lewy body variant of Alzheimer's disease (AD), multiple system atrophy (MSA), and neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation. These humanized wildtype SNCA-GR (gene replacement) knock-in/knock-out mice carry a 158 kb fragment of human SNCA replacing the mouse Snca locus. Mice homozygous for the mutation are viable and fertile with no obvious phenotype. These mice may be useful for studying SNCA function. Of note, the SNCA-GR model set also includes: B6J.B6N-Sncatm2.1(SNCA*A53T)Mdk/J (Stock No. 039167) and B6J.B6N-Sncatm3.1(SNCA*E46K)Mdk/J (Stock No. 039168). 分子注记：The wild -type SNCA-GR targeting vector is designed to insert a 158 kb (Chr4:89707185 - 89865027) fragment of the human SNCA in place of Chr6:60683985 - 60848702 of the mouse Snca gene. The genomic sequences are from human assembly GRCh38.p14 and the mouse assembly GRCm39. The construct includes an loxP-flanked neomycin selection cassette. In addition, there is also a SNCA (non-coding antisense RNA) in the human genome segment. The insertion disrupts Snca transcription. Transient cre-mediated recombination removes the neo cassette."
