      "breeding_summary": "The MS-NASH (formerly called FATZO) inbred strain is maintained by inbreeding. Mating scheme: Sibling x Sibling Coat color: Black Generation: Contact Technical Support",
      "detailed_desc": "MS-NASH (formerly called FATZO) is an inbred polygenic mouse model for studying obesity, metabolic syndrome and diabetes - all in the presumed presence of an intact leptin pathway. On standard diet, MS-NASH males exhibit obesity in a pre-diabetic state that slowly progresses to overt diabetes. MS-NASH females exhibit obesity but do not develop any diabetic phenotype. Approximately ~95% of MS-NASH males become obese (30 grams) by 8 weeks of age. Male obesity is progressive, but not as severe as mice with non-functioning leptin signaling pathways. Glucose intolerance with increased insulin levels is reported in MS-NASH males as young as 6 weeks of age. MS-NASH males become hyperglycemic at ~14 weeks of age, rapidly develop insulin resistance and become hyperinsulinemic (insulin secretion remains at high levels). The early hyperglycemia of MS-NASH males can be accelerated by high fat diets. Furthermore, MS-NASH males maintained on a western diet with fructose develop liver steatosis leading to metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis, or MASH (formerly called NAFLD/NASH). Upon administration of the GLP-1 agonist semaglutide, MS-NASH males respond with a reduction in body weight, food intake and blood glucose. Additional metabolic phenotypes expected from the JAX MSNASH/PcoJ model can be viewed here. Additional characterization of the MS-NASH inbred strain may be available at the Crown Biosciences - Rodent Models of NASH website. The observed coat color is similar to that of C57BL/6J, but there is a small range in coat color from dark brown to black."
