      "breeding_summary": "Both heterozygous and homozygous mice are viable and fertile. Mating scheme: Homozygote x Homozygote Coat color: Black Generation: Contact Technical Support",
      "detailed_desc": "The protein tyrosine phosphatase, receptor type, C, gene (Ptprc; also known as CD45 or Ly5) encodes a protein tyrosine phosphatase expressed on leukocytes and is essential for T and B cell activation. To create the JAXBoy allele (PtprcK302E), CRISPR/Cas9 endonuclease-mediated genome editing of Ptprc exon 10 was used to introduce a K302E amino acid change. This substitution alters the protein structure such that it can be recognized as the Ptprca isoform (CD45.1 or Ly5.1) by monoclonal antibodies (Mercier et al. 2016). The resulting expression of Ptprca rather than the Ptprcb (CD45.2 or Ly5.2) allele normally expressed by C57BL/6J mice makes JAXBoy suitable for use in competitive transplantation studies, either as a donor or host. Further, these mice allow researchers to completely avoid the experimental uncertainties associated with the presence of the SJL inbred strain genetic content found in similar mouse lines (Chisolm et al. 2019). In studies at The Jackson Laboratory, these JAXBoy mice are observed to express CD45.1 in peripheral blood leukocytes (via flow cytometry using commercially available antibodies), both before and after transplantation, in a manner similar to that observed for cells from the C57BL/6-congenic CD45.1 strain Pep Boy (Stock No. 002014) in the same study. Both heterozygous and homozygous mice are viable and fertile. If additional characterization of these JAXBoy mice is performed, we may modify the strain description accordingly. 分子注记：CRISPR/cas9 genome editing is used to alter a lysine to glutamic acid mutation at amino acid 302 (K302E ; AAA to GAA) in exon 10. Additionally, the silent nucleotide change N303N (AAC to AAT) was included for targeting efficiency. The substitution results in the expression of Ptprca (CD45.1 or Ly5.1) rather than the Ptprcb (CD45.2 or Ly5.2) allele normally expressed by C57BL/6J mice."
