      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, homozygous mice may be bred together. Mating scheme: Homozygote x Homozygote Coat color: Black Generation: N2+pN1F1",
      "detailed_desc": "The humanized TfR knock-in mouse model replaces the mouse sequence with human sequence encoding the amino acids that make-up the apical domain (API) of the transferrin receptor gene (Tfrc). The transferrin receptor (TfR) is highly expressed at the blood brain barrier (BBB) and is a transcytosis system that mediates iron delivery to the brain via binding and intracellular trafficking of the iron-binding protein transferrin. Research efforts have focused on utilizing this transcytosis system for delivery of TfR-bound biological therapeutics into the brain. This humanized TfR mouse model was generated where the apical domain of the mouse TfR, which is where all reported TfR and brain-penetrating antibodies bind to, was replaced by the human sequence. Mice that are homozygous for this humanized TfR knock-in allele are viable and fertile, with no overt phenotype. Total TfR protein levels are not significantly different in homozygous humanized TfR brain tissue compared to wildtype controls. The donating laboratory did verify that the mouse apical domain was successfully knocked-out by injecting a mouse specific-apical domain binder, Nb62, fused to the neuropeptide neurotensin and monitoring body temperature. When neurotensin successfully crosses the BBB, the core body temperature is expected to drop. While wildtype mice did demonstrate a significant drop in body temperature after mouse Nb62-neurotensin injection, there was no change in temperature in the homozygous humanized TfR mice, which suggests that the endogenous apical domain has been successfully replaced with human sequence in this model. Likewise, the donating investigator confirmed that the human apical domain in this knock-in model was functional by injecting a human-specific apical domain binder, Nb188, fused to neurotensin. After injection of human Nb188-neurotensin, the homozygous humanized TfR mice demonstrate a significant drop in core body temperature, whereas the wildtype controls did not show any change in temperature. These animals may be a useful tool for evaluating the efficiency and kinetics of human biological therapeutics in neurodegenerative diseases. Note that the donating laboratory indicates some postnatal lethality in heterozygous animals; heterozygous mice may also be smaller in size compared to their wildtype and homozygous littermates. Heterozygotes do continue to gain weight and ultimately show no difference in size compared to wildtype and homozygous mice post-weaning. It is s"
