      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony hemizygous females may be bred to wildtype (noncarrier) males from the colony or to C57BL/6J inbred mice (Stock No. 000664). The donating investigator reports that homozygous mice are viable but are more affected by the neuromuscular impairment than hemizygous animals. Males do not breed well. Line is maintained hemizygous by breeding hemizygous females with wildtype males. Mating scheme: Hemizygote x Noncarrier; Noncarrier x Hemizygote Coat color: Black Generation: N10+pN9\t",
      "detailed_desc": "Please see Important Note for information regarding transgene copy number, as well as the Detailed Description section \"February 2026 Quality Control Findings\" for additional information on transgene copy number and phenotype. Peripheral myelin protein 22 (PMP22) is a tetraspan glycoprotein contained in compact myelin of the peripheral nervous system. Duplication of PMP22 have been associated with the onset of Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A (CMT1A). This age-dependent demyelinating neuropathy is characterized by predominantly distal loss of strength and sensation that often develops in the first 2 decades of life. C3-PMP22 YAC transgenic mice (also called C3-PMP) express a wildtype human peripheral myelin protein 22 (PMP22) gene. As described in Verhamme et al., 2011 J Neuropathol Exp Neurol 70:386, the C3-PMP22 mice were identified by quantitative real-time PCR to have three to four copies of the YAC. The C3-PMP mice showed no overt clinical signs at 3 weeks and developed mild neuromuscular impairment in an age-dependent manner. They had stable, low nerve conduction velocities similar to adults with human CMT1A. Myelination was delayed, and they contained reduced numbers of myelinated fibers at 3 weeks of age. There was no detectable loss of myelinated fibers in adult C3-PMP22 mice. [Verhamme et al., 2011] The donating investigator reported that homozygous mice are viable but are more affected by the neuromuscular impairment than hemizygous animals. Males do not breed well. Line is maintained by breeding hemizygous females with wildtype males. In an attempt to offer this strain with stable transgene copy number similar as described in its primary publication [Verhamme et al., 2011], the Stock No. 030052 distribution colony will be maintained using animals from a high copy (~5 transgene copies) pedigree moving forward [March 2024]. It should be noted that the phenotype could vary from that originally described. We may modify the strain description if necessary as published results become available (e.g., see February 2026 Quality Control Findings below). Please also see Important Note for more information regarding transgene copy number. February 2026 Quality Control Findings by The Jackson Laboratory: &nbsp;&nbsp;In February 2026, additional quality control analyses were concluded by The Jackson Laboratory comparing Stock No. 030052 C3-PMP22 high copy (~5 transgene copies) hemizygous mice with Stock No. 037871 C3-PMP22 low copy (~1 transgene copy) he"
