      "detailed_desc": "K19CreERT mice have a creERT fusion gene (a Cre recombinase fused to a mouse estrogen receptor ligand binding domain) inserted upstream of the initiation codon of the keratin 19 (Krt19) gene. The presence of creERT and a 3' polyadenylation signal abolishes expression of KRT19. K19 is an intermediate filament protein expressed in multiple epithelial cell types from the epiblast stage though adult. Specifically, expression is seen in lung, oral cavity, bronchi, bronchioles, stomach, small intestines, and in the pancreatic, renal, and hepatic ductal cells. Restricted to the cytoplasm, creERT can only gain access to the nuclear compartment after exposure to tamoxifen. When these K19CreERT mice are bred with those containing loxP-flanked sequences, tamoxifen-inducible, Cre-mediated recombination will result in deletion of the floxed sequences in the Cre-expressing cells of the offspring. Publications indicate intraperitoneal injections of tamoxifen may be favorable compared to other methods (e.g., administration via oral gavage). In the absence of tamoxifen, leaky cre activity is detected in less than 1% of stomach and intestinal epithelial cells, but not in pancreatic or hepatic epithelial tissues. Homozygous mice are viable and fertile. 分子注记：An inducible cre/ESR1 fusion gene followed by an frt-flanked neo cassette was inserted upstream of the translation initiation codon. Cre expression is restricted to epithelial cells in multiple endodermal organs (pancreatic ducts, hepatic ducts, stomach and intestine)."
