      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, these mice can be bred as homozygotes. Mating scheme: Homozygote x Homozygote Coat color: Various Generation: ?+pN1F2",
      "detailed_desc": "The actin-related protein encoded by the Actl6b gene (also known as BAF53b) is a subunit of the neuron-specific chromatin remodeling BRG1/brm-associated factor (BAF) complexes. These BAF53b-Cre transgenic mice express Cre recombinase under the control of the mouse Actl6b gene promoter. Cre recombinase expression is detected in the cerebellum and cortex of the brain. Lower levels of Cre recombinase activity is detected in the eye and testis. At E13.5, there is Cre recombinase activity observed in brain and spinal cord, as well as the peripheral nervous system (dorsal root ganglion, sympathetic ganglia, nerve tracts). In postnatal brains of transgenic mice, Cre activity is found in neurons, but not in proliferating neural progenitor cells or glial cells. Cre recombinase activity is also identified in primary cultures of hippocampal and cortical neurons. The Donating Investigator reports that the transgene did not insert into the X chromosome, and that there is no difference in phenotype between hemizygotes and homozygotes. Mice that are homozygous for the transgene are viable and fertile. 分子注记：The mouse bacterial artificial chromosome (BAC) RP23-127E17 containing the entire Actl6b gene and approximately 100 kb flanking sequence, was modified by replacing the first 2 exons of the Actl6b gene with the PCR product of the cre recombinase gene and Actl6b 3' UTR, which situates the cre gene just downstream of the start codon. Founder line 4092 was subsequently established."
