      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, these mice can be bred as homozygotes. Of note, The Jackson Laboratory live colony of Ki67RFP knock-in mice shows that homozygotes exhibit white belly spots in all pedigree lines. Because our colony is maintained by breeding homozygotes together, is not known if heterozygous mice have these spots [October 2019]. Mating scheme: Homozygote x Homozygote Coat color: Black Generation: N3+pN1F2pN1",
      "detailed_desc": "The targeted Mki67 gene encodes a nuclear protein that is a marker for proliferating cells. These mice carry a red fluorescent protein knock-in allele of the Mki67 gene. Red fluorescence is detected in the spleen, thymus, brain, hair follicles, bone marrow, eye, liver, pancreas, small intestine and colon. Most actively dividing cells in small intestinal crypts express red fluorescent protein. Isolated and sorted red fluorescent intestinal crypt cells form organoid cultures. Mice that are homozygous for the targeted mutation are viable and fertile. The Donating Investigator reports that the presence or absence of the neo cassette does not alter the phenotype of the mice. Of note, The Jackson Laboratory live colony of Ki67RFP knock-in mice shows that homozygotes exhibit white belly spots in all pedigree lines. Because our colony is maintained by breeding homozygotes together, is not known if heterozygous mice have these spots [October 2019]. 分子注记：A TagRFP reporter was inserted in frame in the C terminus of the gene via homologous recombination. This mutation creates a C-terminal fusion ofTagRFP to the endogenous protein. Flp-mediated recombination removed the FRT-flanked neo cassette. Reporter expression is detected in actively dividing epithelial cells."
