      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, homozygous mice may be bred together. Mating scheme: Homozygote x Homozygote Coat color: Black Generation: N7+pN2F3",
      "detailed_desc": "Foxp2 (forkhead box P2) is a transcription factor that marks a subset of retinal ganglion cells (F-RGCs) that vary in size, distribution, and selectivity to directional motion. These targeted mice express IRES-cre from the mouse Foxp2 gene. When crossed with floxed reporter strain, resultant pups demonstrate widespread expression of cre recombinase, perhaps reflecting broad expression of Foxp2 during early development. F-RGCs and their projections can be more specifically labeled in the mature retina by intravitreal injection of adeno-associated virus (e.g. AAV2/9Flex-GFP) to deliver a cre-dependent GFP cassette. A nuclear localizing signal (NLS) directs expression of cre to the nucleus. A Myc-tag feature has not been tested. Although incorporated in the targeting construct, GFP fluorescence is too low to be visualized directly or through immunohistochemistry. PCR genotyping analysis at The Jackson Laboratory has failed to detect a GFP component. Heterozygotes are viable, fertile, and show no overt phenotype as the coding region of the gene is unaffected. Expression of the targeted gene is presumed to be intact. 分子注记：IRES-Myc tag-nuclear localization signal (NLS)-cre-GFP-frt-neomycin-frt were introduced just after the termination codon of the mouse Foxp2 gene via homologous recombination. The neomycin cassette was excised through crosses with animals broadly expressing Flp recombinase. The GFP is believed to be nonfunctional."
