      "breeding_summary": "The GT11ML/TG11ML (MADM-ML-11GT/TG) compound heterozygous mice harbor reciprocal mutations on homologous chromosomes (\"trans-heterozygous\"). Specifically, the GT11ML targeted mutation (MADM-ML-11GT ; Igs2tm1(ACTB-EGFP,-tdTomato)Zng) is on one homologous chromosome, and the TG11ML targeted mutation (MADM-ML-11TG ; Igs2tm2(ACTB-tdTomato,-EGFP)Zng) is on the other homologous chromosome. When maintaining a live colony, compound heterozygous mice may be bred together. To date (July 2017), The Jackson Laboratory colony of the two parental lines (GT11ML and TG11ML) have shown coat colors of black, grey or agouti, and black, respectively. Mating scheme: Compound Heterozygous for Igs2tm1(ACTB-EGFP-tdTomato)Zng Igs2tm2(ACTB-tdTomato-EGFP)Zng X Wild-type for Igs2tm1(ACTB-EGFP-tdTomato)Zng Igs2tm2(ACTB-tdTomato-EGFP)Zng and reciprocal. Coat color: Various Generation: ?p+F2pN1",
      "detailed_desc": "The GT11ML/TG11ML (MADM-ML-11GT/TG) compound heterozygous mice harbor reciprocal mutations on homologous chromosomes (\"trans-heterozygous\"). Specifically, the GT11ML targeted mutation (MADM-ML-11GT ; Igs2tm1(ACTB-EGFP,-tdTomato)Zng) is on one homologous chromosome, and the TG11ML targeted mutation (MADM-ML-11TG ; Igs2tm2(ACTB-tdTomato,-EGFP)Zng) is on the other homologous chromosome. Each respective allele has the CMV enhancer/chicken beta-actin core promoter upstream of reciprocal portions of mut4-EGFP and MYC-tagged tdTomato separated by a beta-globin intronic sequence (containing frt-lox5171-lox2272-frt-loxP-Neo-loxP) - all inserted into the Igs2 locus (Hipp11 or MADM-11; an intergenic region near the centromere of chromosome 11. GT11ML/TG11ML mice are viable and fertile with no gross behavioral or observable abnormalities. These GT11ML/TG11ML trans-heterozygotes are designed for MADM (mosaic analysis with double markers), and must be bred to harbor a Cre- or FLP-recombinase to induce fluorescent protein expression. Prior to Cre- or FLP-recombination, trans-heterozygous mutant mice do not have colored cells: the chimeric genes do not produce functional proteins because their coding sequences are interrupted by the beta-globin intron in different reading frames. After DNA replication (G2 phase) in double mutant mice, introduction of Cre- or FLP-recombinase that facilitates inter-chromosomal recombination aligns the respective N- and C-terminal coding sequences for each of the reporter genes on the same chromosome. The subsequent chromatid segregation (X or Z) determines daughter cell phenotype: recombinant sister chromatids into the same daughter cell (a G2-Z event) leads to double reporter expression or no reporter expression, while independent segregation into separate daughter cells (a G2-X event) leads to expression of either EGFP or tdTomato-MYC. If an additional targeted mutation of interest is introduced distal to the Igs2 locus on chromosome 11, only homozygous cells will be singly labeled following G2 cre or FLP introduction. The homozygous mutant and wildtype cells can then be distinguished by which single reporter they express. Most heterozygous cells will be unlabeled, but some heterozygous cells will be yellow (both markers expressed). Reporter protein tissue specificity, expression levels, and frequency of recombination are thus determined by the promoter controlling Cre- or FLP-recombinase expression. Using this MADM system, a researcher c"
