      "breeding_summary": "Heterozygotes are viable and fertile with no reported gross physical or behavioral abnormalities. In March 2026, the donating laboratory states that TIGRE knock-in alleles with long stretch of repetitive sequence and multiple transcription units could potentially lead to instability and unequal cross-over during meiosis. As such, the donating laboratory strongly recommends maintaining this strain as heterozygous. When maintaining a live colony, heterozygous mice may be bred together, to wildtype mice from the colony or to C57BL/6J inbred mice (Stock No. 000664). Mating scheme: Wild-type x Heterozygote; Heterozygote x Wild-type Coat color: Black Generation: N7+pN1F6\t",
      "detailed_desc": "Ai162(TIT2L-GC6s-ICL-tTA2)-D (also called Ai162D) mice harbor the TIGRE-Ins-TRE2-LSL-GCaMP6s-Ins-CAG-LSL-tTA2 conditional allele, designed with a TRE2 promoter, loxP-flanked STOP cassette and GCaMP6s sequence, and then a CAG promoter, lox2272-flanked STOP cassette and tTA2 sequence. Prior to Cre recombinase exposure, this allele is designed to have the two floxed-STOP cassettes preventing expression of the downstream genes (GCaMP6s and tTA2). When bred to mice expressing Cre recombinase, the offspring will have both floxed-STOP cassettes deleted in the cre-expressing cells/tissues - resulting in GCaMP6s expression and tTA2 expression. When GCaMP6s is expressed, subsequent calcium binding (such as neuronal activation) results in robust EGFP fluorescence. Further administration of tetracycline (or its analog doxycycline [dox]) will directly inhibit tTA2 activity, and therefore is expected to reduce GCaMP6s expression levels. Specifically, the donating investigator reports Ai162D exhibit no native GCaMP6s fluorescence in the absence of Cre. When Ai162D mice are bred with Cre-driver lines to create double transgenic animals (Ai162D+/Cre+), cells expressing both tTA2 and Cre exhibit baseline GCaMP6s fluorescence in the absence of calcium binding. Following calcium binding (such as neuronal activation), a robust increase in GCaMP6s fluorescence is observed. To date (January 2018), Ai162D animals have not been characterized regarding dox-inducible attenuation of GCaMP6s expression, nor the Cre-inducible Ai162D expression in tissues other than brain. For example, combining Ai162 with Slc17a7-IRES2-Cre (see Stock No. 023527) generates double transgenic Slc17a7-IRES2-Cre;Ai162 animals with strong GCaMP6s expression in pan-cortical glutamatergic neurons (and no aberrant [interictal] activity in animals tested to date [n=7; January 2018]). The same may be expected for Ai162D. The donating investigator reports several examples where Ai162 crosses to neocortical layer-specific Cre-driver lines (e.g., Calb1-IRES2-Cre [see Stock No. 028532]) or interneuron Cre-driver lines (e.g., Sst-IRES2-Cre [see Stock No. 013044]) result in double transgenic mice with robust native GCaMP6s expression. The same may be expected for Ai162D. Additionally, the donating investigator reports crossing Ai162 with the pan-excitatory Emx1-IRES-Cre line (Stock No. 005628) or the pan-inhibitory Gad2-IRES-Cre line (Stock No. 010802) may generate offspring with high levels of CAG-driven tTA2 expressi"
