      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, heterozygous mice may be bred to wildtype mice from the colony or to C57BL/6J inbred mice (Stock No. 000664). Homozygotes are not viable. Mating scheme: Heterozygote x Wild-type; Wild-type x Heterozygote Coat color: Black Generation: N?+pN4F1",
      "detailed_desc": "Pdgfr&alpha;-CreERT2 mice have a cre/ERT2 fusion gene inserted into the start codon of the platelet derived growth factor receptor, alpha polypeptide (Pdgfra) gene. Insertion of this fusion gene abolishes Pdgfr&alpha; gene expression. Pdgfr&alpha; encodes a cell surface tyrosine kinase receptor expressed in a variety of cell types. It is involved in organ development, differentiation of bone marrow-derived mesenchymal cells, and normal development of the mucosa lining the gastrointestinal tract and development of the lung. It also plays a role in cell migration, chemotaxis in wound healing, and tumor progression. Mice that are heterozygous for this knock-in/knock-out allele are viable and fertile, while homozygotes are not viable. When Pdgfr&alpha;-CreERT2 mice are bred with mice containing loxP-flanked sequence(s), tamoxifen-inducible Cre-mediated recombination will result in deletion of the floxed sequence(s) in the Cre-expressing cells of the offspring. Cre recombination occurs in Pdgfra+ fibroblasts in many tissues in the developing embryo and adult mouse after tamoxifen administration. &nbsp; 分子注记：The targeting vector contained an cre/ERT2 cassette inserted at the start codon of the Pdgfra locus. The cre cassette was followed by a FRT-flanked neo cassette, which was removed by flp-mediated excision after germline transmission."
