      "breeding_summary": "Since the gene of interest is X-linked, homozygous females may be bred to hemizygous males. Mating scheme: Heterozygote x Hemizygote Coat color: Black Generation: N12+pN3F5",
      "detailed_desc": "&Delta;dblGATA knock-out mice have the double GATA-site 21 bp upstream of the first hematopoietic exon of the X-linked GATA binding protein 1 (Gata1) gene replaced with a loxP site. Homozygous females and hemizygous males are viable and fertile. This mutation results in the complete ablation of the eosinophil lineage, even under conditions that normally stimulate eosinophil development, without affecting the development of other GATA-1 dependent lineages (erythroid, megakaryocytic, and mast cells). Expression of the endogenous gene is observed in erythroid and bone marrow cells. This mutant may be useful for in vivo studies of eosinophil function and eosinophil-related pathologies, including asthma and pulmonary physiology. Of note, this allele is also available on a congenic BALB/cJ background as Stock No. 005653. In an attempt to offer alleles on well-characterized or multiple genetic backgrounds, alleles are frequently moved to a genetic background different from that on which an allele was first characterized. It should be noted that the phenotype of these C57BL/6J-congenic mice could vary from that originally described on a BALB/c genetic background. We may modify the strain description if necessary as published results become available. 分子注记：A high-affinity, palindromic \"double\" GATA protein binding site in the Gata1 promoter presumed to mediate positive Gata1 autoregulation was replaced by a floxed Pgk-neo cassette; transient Cre recombinase expression in ES cells left a single loxP site flanked by two Not1 sites. The 21-bp deleted segment comprised nucleotides -691 through -671 upstream of the last nucleotide of the first hematopoietically expressed exon of Gata1."
