      "detailed_desc": "Human papillomavirus type 16 (HPV16) is a high-risk small DNA tumor virus that causes papillomas in skin, genitalia, and the upper respiratory tract. HPV16 is associated with cervical cancer.&nbsp; Cancer cells found in HPV-infected individuals often have the HPV genome integrated into the cellular genome. The integration includes the early region of the HPV genome containing the E6 and E7 genes. In cell cultures E6 can immortalize primary human mammary epithelial cells and E7 can transform primary human keratinocytes. E6 has been found to associate with TRP53 and lead to TRP53 degradation through the ubiquitin proteasome pathway. E7 has been found to associate with RB (retinoblastoma) and interfere with RB binding and inactivation of the E2F transcription factor.K14E6 transgenic (high copy founder 5737) mice carry a human keratin 14 (KRT14) promoter directing expression of the E6 gene from human papillomavirus type 16 (HPV16). The KRT14 promoter directs expression to the basal layer of the epithelia. Mice homozygous for the transgene are viable and fertile.&nbsp; Homozygous K14E6 mice develop hyperproliferation and epidermal hyperplasia resulting in thickened ears. Mice also exhibit cataracts and scruffy fur. Skin tumors in the torso and limbs develop in 14% of mice at 15 months of age. &nbsp;Most tumors found in K14E6 mice are malignant.Of note, the K14E7 transgenic strain&nbsp;(expressing HPV16 E7) is distributed as FVB/N-Tg(KRT14-HPV16E7)2304Plam/J (Stock No. 037879). 分子注记：A human papilloma virus 16 (HPV16) genomic fragment (nt79-883) encompassing both the E6 and E7 genes was cloned between the human KRT14 promoter and polyA sequences. Only E6 is expressed due to the presence of a translation termination linker in the N-terminal region of the E7 gene. This line was estimated by Southern blot analysis to carry approximately 15 copies of the transgene. In situ hybridization demonstrates expession of E6 mRNA in the epidermis and also in the dermis, but confined largely to hair follicles in the latter."
