      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, hemizygous mice may be bred to non-carrier littermates or to C57BL/6J. Of note, the donating laboratory maintains the colony by breeding C57BL/6J females to hemizygous males. Although platelet counts are decreased in these transgenic mice, no bleeding abnormalities have been noted by the donating investigator. Mating scheme: Noncarrier x Hemizygote Coat color: Black Generation: +pN1",
      "detailed_desc": "B cell leukemia/lymphoma 2 (BCL2) is a protein that is primarily involved in regulating the intrinsic (mitochondrial) apoptosis pathway. Overexpression of BCL2 activity is a feature of many cancers, such as follicular lymphoma and chronic lymphocytic lymphoma. Increased BCL2 results in the inhibition of cell death. VavP-BCL-2 mice express a transgene encoding a full-length human BCL2 cDNA driven by the enhancer/promoter of the vav gene (Vav), an intron sequence from the SR&alpha; expression vector, and the SV40 late region polyadenylation signal (Ogilvy et al. Blood (1999)). Transgene expression is pan-hematopoietic. Hemizygous VavP-BCL-2 transgenic mice are viable and fertile; the donating laboratory has not attempted to produce homozygous VavP-BCL-2 mice. Young hemizygous mice display increased myeloid cells, a marked elevation in B and T lymphocytes, splenomegaly, expansion of lymphoid follicles and reduced blood platelets (Ogilvy et al. PNAS (1999)) and go on to develop autoimmune kidney disease and follicular lymphoma (Egle et al. Blood (2004)). These VavP-BCL-2 transgenic mice may be useful for studying the role of BCL2 in hematopoiesis, apoptosis, leukemia/lymphoma development and drug responsiveness/resistance. Of note, the insertion site(s) and copy number have not been determined for this transgenic line. Although platelet counts are decreased in these transgenic mice, no bleeding abnormalities have been noted by the donating investigator. 分子注记：Overexpression of human BCL2 cDNA was driven by the mouse Vav promoter, which drives expression predominantly in nucleated cells of hematopoietic tissue."
