      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, homozygous mice may be bred together. Mating scheme: Homozygote x Homozygote Coat color: Black Generation: N8+pN1F1",
      "detailed_desc": "Epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) is a member of the receptor tyrosine kinase family and involved in the regulation of epithelial cell growth and maintenance. Mutations in the Egfr gene have been associated with lung cancers, specifically non-small cell lung cancers. The most common Egfr mutations in human patients are L858R missense mutations and in-frame exon 19 deletions. These Egfrdel19 knock-in mice have a stop cassette flanked by two loxP sites inserted into intron 1 of the Gt(ROSA)26Sor locus. When bred to mice that express tissue-specific Cre recombinase, resulting offspring will have overexpression of the Egfrdel19 mutant in cre-expressing tissues. These mice will also express mCerulean3 fluorescent protein after Cre-mediated recombination. Homozygous Egfrdel19 mice are viable and fertile, with no overt phenotype. The donating laboratory crossed these Egfrdel19 knock-in mice to p53LoxP mice (Stock No. 008462) and the resulting progeny were exposed to an adeno-Cre virus (AdCre) via intratracheal administration. Approximately 8-14 weeks after AdCre exposure, hyperplasias, atypical adenomatous hyperplasias (AAH), adenomas, and multifocal adenocarcinomas were noted in all Egfrdel19 heterozygous mice. mCerulean3 (blue) fluorescence is detectable in tumor cells, after Cre-mediated recombination, by direct fluorescence. These Egfrdel19 knock-in mice may be useful for studying lung cancer onset and progression as well as potential lung cancer therapeutics. Note that the donating laboratory has also made a Cre-inducible EgfrL860R mouse line available, Stock No. 038454. 分子注记：The targeting cassette includes (from 5&rsquo; to 3&rsquo;) a CMV-IE enhancer/chicken beta-actin/rabbit beta-globin hybrid promoter (CAG), a loxP-flanked STOP cassette including neomycin sequence, Egfr mutant cDNA (exon 19 deletion), mCerulean3 (mC3) sequence, a woodchuck hepatitis virus post-transcriptional regulatory element (WPRE; to enhance the mRNA transcript stability), and polyA signal. This targeting vector was inserted into intron 1 of the Gt(ROSA)26Sor locus."
