      "breeding_summary": "Heterozygotes are viable and fertile. Approximately 25% of homozygous neonates suffer from spontaneous hemorrhaging events which resolve in many of the animals. Approximately 50% of homozygotes die as neonates, but females are noted to show better survival than males. Homozygous males can breed. Homozygous females bleed after giving birth and die. Mating scheme: Heterozygote x Wild-type; Wild-type x Heterozygote Coat color: Black Generation: N8+pN1",
      "detailed_desc": "FGS- mice carry a targeted knock-out allele of the mouse fibrinogen gamma chain (Fgg; FG) gene in which all of the coding exons were replaced by a neomycin cassette. Homozygous animals do not express any detectable plasma fibrinogen. Approximately 25% of homozygous neonates suffer from spontaneous hemorrhaging events. Approximately 50% of homozygotes die as neonates, but females are noted to show better survival than males. The bleeding is primarily abdominal but is also observed in the cranial, neck, and nasal areas. Although these events initially seem dramatic, many homozygous mice are able to resolve the bleeding and recover. Thrombin-induced clot times are significantly delayed in homozygotes. This strain has also been useful in demonstrating that fibrinogen is not essential for the development of bleomycin-induced pulmonary fibrosis. Heterozygotes do not present an overt phenotype. 分子注记：The entire coding region of the endogenous locus was replaced with a neomycin selection cassette. Western blot analysis of blood plasma showed the absence of the gamma polypeptide, preventing the formation of the fibrinogen heterodimer in homozygous mutant mice."
