      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, heterozygous mice may be bred together. The donating investigator did not maintain a homozygous colony. It is unknown if homozygotes are viable and fertile (February 2024). Mating scheme: Heterozygote x Wild-type; Wild-type x Heterozygote Coat color: Black Generation: N4+pN1F5",
      "detailed_desc": "CRISPR/Cas9 generated Dpp4-creERT2 knock-in mice have cre-ERT2-P2A inserted between the 5' UTR and exon 1 of the dipeptidylpeptidase 4 (Dpp4) gene. The insertion of cre-ERT2-P2A does not affect endogenous gene expression. These mice express cre-ERT2 in arachnoid barrier cells (ABCs). The arachnoid barrier is a component of the blood-cerebrospinal fluid barrier (B-CSFB) in the meninges and is composed of epithelial-like, tight-junction-expressing cells. The arachnoid barrier is positioned immediately beneath the dural border cells and forms a B-CSFB between the dura mater and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) in the subarachnoid space (SAS). Restricted to the cytoplasm, cre-ERT2 can only gain access to the nuclear compartment after exposure to tamoxifen. Heterozygotes are viable and fertile with no overt phenotype. The donating investigator did not maintain a homozygous colony, it is unknown if homozygotes are viable and fertile (February 2024). When these mice are bred with mice containing loxP-flanked sequence, tamoxifen-inducible Cre-mediated recombination will result in deletion of the floxed sequences in the Cre-expressing cells of the offspring. For example, when bred to Ai6(RCL-ZsGreen) mice (Stock No. 007906), recombination is evident in polygonal cells tiling the brain surface after tamoxifen induction. No cre activity is detected prior to tamoxifen administration in the brain/dura. The donating investigator also made a similar mouse line, Slc47a1-creERT2 mice (Stock No. 039218), which express cre/ERT2 in dural border cells (DBCs) of the brain. 分子注记：Using CRISPR/Cas9 technology, a cre/ERT2 fusion gene with a P2A self-cleaving peptide were inserted between the 5' UTR and exon 1 of theDpp4 gene."
