      "breeding_summary": "The Atlas&trade; Full Human Diversity Mouse (Stock No. 039317) is distributed as F1 (first filial generation) mice resulting from breeding the double homozygous Fully-Hu KLC :: Fully human HC strain (B6;129-Igkem1(IGK*)Abtx Ighem1(IGH*)Abtx/J) with the double homozygous KLaP (k-KO) :: mIgDJ_D KO strain (B6;129-Igkem2Abtx Ighem2Abtx/J). The resulting F1 mice are compound heterozygous at the Igk and Igh loci (each allele is described in the Development section). These F1 mice are viable and fertile with no gross phenotypic abnormalities reported.",
      "detailed_desc": "Atlas&trade; Full Human Diversity Mice contain targeted knock-ins of the human heavy and kappa light V(D)J regions in place of the endogenous variable region of the murine immunoglobulin loci. By containing the near full complement of human antibody gene sequence diversity with antibody titers comparable to wildtype mice, Atlas&trade; Mouse antibodies are selected for and matured in vivo, simplifying downstream engineering and streamlining the discovery and development process. This unique platform exhibits the improved speed, diversity, developability, and affinity that are core to knock-in antibody mice. Key Benefits: In vivo selection and maturation of antibodies resulting in higher numbers of developable lead antibody candidates with high affinity and fewer requirements for ex vivo engineering compared to in vitro display and wildtype mouse platforms.Variable regions comprising &gt;99% of commonly expressed human sequences reduce the risk of immunogenicity and improve antibody developability characteristics by limiting the need for downstream engineering.Knock-in format into endogenous mouse loci allows for physiologically-relevant expression and generation of antibody sequences.Use of only functional variable heavy chain (VH) and kappa light chain (VK) gene segments, without pseudogenes or duplicates, promotes more productive B-cell immunoglobulin gene rearrangements. 分子注记：The allele has the mouse immunoglobulin kappa chain complex (Igk) region variable (V) and joining (J) segments deleted and replaced with a human IGK V-J sequence. Specifically, CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing in 129S6/SvEvTac-derived CMTI-1 embryonic stem (ES) cells was used in two separate steps to deliver into the mouse Igk locus a ~108 kb sequence comprising human IGK V-J segments (a synthetic DNA construct containing an array of 34 IGK V segments plus all 5 IGK J segments). The allele has the mouse immunoglobulin kappa chain complex (Igk) region variable (V) and joining (J) segments deleted. Specifically, CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing in 129S6/SvEvTac-derived CMTI-1 embryonic stem (ES) cells was used to remove a ~3.2 Mb sequence including the mouse Igk V and J segments and replace it with a PGK-neomycin resistance cassette. The allele has the mouse immunoglobulin heavy chain complex (Igh) region variable (V), diversity (D) and joining (J) segments deleted and replaced with a human IGH V-D-J sequence. Specifically, CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing in 129S6/SvEvTac-derived CMTI-1 embryonic stem"
