      "breeding_summary": "When maintaining a live colony, heterozygous mice may be bred to wildtype mice from the colony or to C57BL/6J inbred mice (Stock No. 000664). Mating scheme: Heterozygote x Heterozygote Coat color: Black Generation: +pN1",
      "detailed_desc": "The targeted Shank3 gene encodes a synaptic scaffolding protein, expressed in the postsynaptic density of excitatory synapses. Mutations in this gene have been associated with autism spectrum disorders and schizophrenia. These Shank3&Delta;e4-22 knock-out mice have protein-coding exons 4 through 22 deleted, no SHANK3 isoforms are generated. No gene product (protein) is detected by western blot analysis of whole brain lysate using C-terminal and N-terminal Shank3 antibodies, and no transcript is detected by RT-PCR. Homozygotes are viable and fertile with the phenotype described below. Neonatal homozygotes have delayed ear opening and paw position. Homozygotes exhibit repetitive behavior (excessive grooming), emit fewer ultrasonic vocalizations, motor coordination deficits, increased startle reflexes, abnormal socialization behaviors, and learning deficits. In new environments, knock-out mice display increased escape behaviors. At 4 to 7 months of age half of homozygous mice exhibit skin lesions that may be due to excessive grooming. These mice may be useful for studying behavioral abnormalities associated with Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) and Phelan-McDermid syndrome. Of note, the donating investigator has donated floxed Shank3e4-22 flox conditional knock-out mice (Stock No. 039525) and Shank3e4-9 knock-in/knock-out mice (Stock No. 017442). 分子注记：A loxP site was inserted into intron 3 and an FRT site flanked neomycin resistance gene cassette and a second loxP site were inserted into intron 9. Subsquently, an FRT site flanked puromycin resistance gene cassette and a third loxP site were inserted downstream of exon 22. Subsequent flp-mediated recombination removed the two selection cassettes and cre-mediated recombination deleted exons 4 to 22, creating a null allele."
