      "breeding_summary": "When maintained as a live colony, heterozygous males younger than 9 months of age may be bred to wildtype females. The donating investigators have not tried breeding for homozygosity. Homozygous mice are functionally Trp53 knock-outs in all tissues and will develop tumors. Mating scheme: Heterozygote x Wild-type; Wild-type x Heterozygote Generation: +pN1",
      "detailed_desc": "OvTrpMyc knock-in/knock-out mice are a spontaneous tumor model of ovarian cancer and uterine serous carcinoma. The Ovgp1 promoter drives constitutive expression of the dominant-negative gain-of-function mutant form of Trp53 R270H and nuclear Myc oncogene. This mutation replaces the native mouse Trp53 coding region. Homozygotes are functionally a Trp53 knock-out in all tissues, not just Ovgp1-driven tissues. Heterozygous males and females are viable and fertile. In both sexes, cancer develops as early as 9 months of age, with a median onset of 15 months. Female mice developed lethal cancer at an average of 14.5 months. Histopathologically, mice reveal high-grade serous ovarian cancer (HGSOC) characteristics, including nuclear TRP53 and nuclear MYC in clusters of cells within the fallopian tube epithelium and ovarian surface epithelium. The model is useful in studies for HGSOC, particularly without requiring BRCA or PTEN to drive disease. 分子注记：Trp53 (NM_001127233.1) containing an R270H mutation and Myc (NM_001177352.1) were knocked into the endogenous Trp53 locus in a reverse orientation using CRISPR-Cas9Ã¢Â€Â“mediated gene editing."
