      "breeding_summary": "Heterozygous mice are viable and fertile. Homozygous mice are viable but exhibit high mortality, around 40% of homozygous FHR/R mice die by 16 weeks of age. The donating investigator suggests breeding heterozygous mice together. Mating scheme: Heterozygote x Wild-type; Wild-type x Heterozygote Generation: +pN1",
      "detailed_desc": "FHR knock-in mice have a tryptophan to arginine mutation at amino acid 1206 (W1206R) of the complement component factor h (Cfh) gene. Cfh encodes a serum glycoprotein that is involved in the regulation of the alternative pathway of the complement system of innate immunity. The complement pathway plays an essential role in the migration of cells during development and in innate and adaptive immune responses that help the body clear foreign material by recognizing pathogens and neutralizing them. In cells and in circulation, CFH inhibits the activation of C3 to C3a and C3b and inactivates existing C3b. This inhibits the formation of the membrane attack complex and lysis of the target cell, both of which contribute to tissue injury and pathogen recognition and elimination. Cfh is associated with age-related macular degeneration (AMD). Western blot analysis of plasma from homozygous FHR/R shows higher FH protein levels (115%) than the normal protein levels in wild-type mice. FHR/R mice are smaller and thinner than homozygous wildtype (FHW/W) and heterozygous (FHW/R) littermates from 4 to 18 weeks of age. 30% of FHR/R mice exhibit severe neurological abnormalities, including hindlimb paralysis and circular movement with most of these mice showing ischemic brain injury and the presence of thrombi and/or intracerebral hemorrhage in the brain. Also, 48% of FHR/R mice die by 30 weeks of age due to severe thrombocytopenia involving multiple organs, including liver, lung, spleen, and kidney and anemia that is evident at 4 and 8 weeks of age, which leads to renal failure, stroke, and retinopathy. These mice have a normal white blood cell count, low hemoglobin (Hb) levels, and an increased reticulocyte count. Heterozygous mice are viable and fertile. Homozygous mice are viable but exhibit high mortality; around 58.5% of homozygous FHR/R mice die by 16 weeks of age. The donating investigator suggests breeding heterozygous mice together. 分子注记：A T to A single nucleotide mutation was introduced by site-directed mutagenesis resulting in a tryptophan (W) to arginine (R) mutation at amino acid 1206. An FRT-flanked neomycin selection cassette was inserted upstream of exon 22 and was removed via Flp-mediated recombination. The W1206R change in mouse corresponds to the W1183R mutation found in families with atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome. Western blot analysis of plasma indicates that protein levels are higher (115%) than the normal protein levels in wild-type mice."
